  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual landscape-to-ocean diagram showing the sources, transport, transformation, and storage of black carbon (BC) across Earth’s surface systems. Fires on land generate BC in soils and release it to the atmosphere as smoke, which is then transported and aged before being redeposited as aerosol BC onto land or water. On hillslopes and in soils, the diagram highlights uncertain fates such as reburning, decomposition, microbial loss, and export by water and wind erosion.\n\nBC moves downslope into freshwater and sediment pools, then through “intermediate reservoirs” and river networks as riverine BC. Along this path, several possible processes are marked, including incorporation into soil, mineralization, flocculation, and groundwater transport, suggesting repeated cycling and partial retention within the hydrological system. Some BC is deposited internally within sediments rather than reaching the sea.\n\nIn the marine environment, BC can undergo further changes, including possible ultraviolet oxidation, interaction with particles that sink, sediment resuspension, and loss through primary aerosol formation back to the atmosphere. The figure emphasizes that BC is not a simple one-way flux but a complex cycle linking terrestrial burning, atmospheric movement, inland waters, rivers, marine systems, and sediments, with multiple uncertain transformation pathways.",
